---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ToolCall from "../components/ToolCall.astro";
import { transcript, executeCall, fanoutCall } from "../data/scenario";
---

<Base
  title="harness-dispatch"
  description="Delegate coding tasks to the agent CLIs you already pay for. harness-dispatch routes each task to the best-fit harness (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Antigravity, or any endpoint), spending subscription quota first — metered and unknown-billing routes stay blocked until you opt in. Local MCP server, MIT."
>
  <header>
    <div class="wrap nav">
      <a class="brand" href="#top">
        <span class="brand-mark">HD</span>
        harness-dispatch
      </a>
      <ul class="nav-links">
        <li><a href="#how">How it works</a></li>
        <li><a href="#install">Install</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        <li><a class="gh" href="https://github.com/fstubner/harness-dispatch">GitHub →</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="top">
    <div class="wrap hero">
      <h1>Delegate to the coding agents you already pay for.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        harness-dispatch turns the coding harnesses on your machine
        (<strong>Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Antigravity</strong>) and any local or
        remote OpenAI-compatible endpoint into tools any AI can call. Each task
        routes to the best-fit agent, spending flat-rate subscription quota first —
        raw metered keys and unknown-billing routes stay blocked until you opt in.
        Long tasks run as background jobs; nothing is ever lost to a timeout.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install →</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/fstubner/harness-dispatch">View source</a>
      </div>

      <div class="transcript" aria-label="Example agent session using harness-dispatch">
        <p class="t-user">{transcript.userPrompt}</p>
        <p class="t-agent">{transcript.agentIntro}</p>
        <div class="t-pending">
          <span class="t-spin" aria-hidden="true">⠋</span>
          {transcript.pendingLine}
        </div>
        <p class="t-note">{transcript.waitNote}</p>
        <ToolCall call={executeCall} />
        <p class="t-agent">{transcript.afterExecute}</p>
        <ToolCall call={fanoutCall} />
        <p class="t-agent">{transcript.closing}</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section id="how">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="section-head">
          <div>
            <span class="label">01 — How it works</span>
            <h2>Not every task wants the same agent.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>
            Codex is good at grinding through execution with its own test loop. Claude
            tends to plan and architect better. Cursor has editor-aware context built in.
            harness-dispatch matches the task to whichever fits, then hands off to that
            agent's own CLI. No proxy in between.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat">
            <h3>Task type</h3>
            <p>Execute, plan, review — each has a different best-fit agent.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat">
            <h3>Billing policy</h3>
            <p>Metered or unknown-billing routes are blocked until you opt in.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat">
            <h3>Live quota</h3>
            <p>Routes with available quota are preferred; rate-limited ones are skipped.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat">
            <h3>Safety profile</h3>
            <p>read_only, workspace_edit, full_auto gate what an agent can touch.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="install">
      <div class="wrap two-col">
        <div>
          <span class="label">02 — Setup</span>
          <h2>Configure is the entrypoint.</h2>
          <p class="section-note">
            One command finds what's installed, checks it actually works, and writes
            the config for you.
          </p>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li>Detect Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Antigravity, and configured endpoints.</li>
            <li>Verify availability and auth without spending quota where possible.</li>
            <li>Choose billing policy, safety profile, and workspace policy per route.</li>
            <li>Write config, print an MCP snippet, connect your agent.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="code">
            <div class="code-head">setup</div>
            <pre><span class="prompt">npm install -g harness-dispatch</span>
<span class="prompt">harness-dispatch configure</span>
<span class="c">Detected 4 harness routes.</span>
<span class="prompt">harness-dispatch doctor --live</span>
<span class="c">ok routes: 1 ready route(s)
ok live-probe: routed one prompt via codex
note 3 route(s) blocked pending allow_paid_usage</span></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="code">
            <div class="code-head">mcp config</div>
            <pre>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "harness-dispatch": { "command": "harness-dispatch" }
  }
}`}</pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="surface">
      <div class="wrap">
        <span class="label">03 — Surface</span>
        <h2 class="surface-title">MCP for agents. REST for everything else.</h2>
        <div class="iface-grid">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>MCP</h3>
            <p><strong>Tools:</strong> <code>dispatch</code> <code>job_status</code> <code>usage</code></p>
            <p><strong>Resources:</strong> <code>harness-dispatch://status</code></p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>REST</h3>
            <p><code>POST /v1/chat/completions</code></p>
            <p><code>GET /v1/status</code></p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Local endpoints</h3>
            <p>Direct: Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM, LiteLLM.</p>
            <p>Native: Codex with local providers.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="faq">
      <div class="wrap">
        <span class="label">04 — FAQ</span>
        <h2 class="surface-title">Short answers.</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why local only?</h3>
            <p>Nothing phones home. Traces (if enabled) go to localhost unless you point them elsewhere. The one outbound call is a public leaderboard GET for route scoring.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why require opt-in for paid routes?</h3>
            <p>Routes with no provider-side backstop — a raw metered API key, or unknown billing — stay blocked until you set <code>allow_paid_usage: true</code> on it. Subscription overage on the provider's own side (Cursor on-demand billing, Claude usage credits, Codex flexible credits) isn't something harness-dispatch can detect or block — if your account has that enabled, it applies exactly as it would outside this tool.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>How is this different from a model proxy?</h3>
            <p>Proxies swap which model answers your agent's next request. harness-dispatch swaps which agent runs the task; each harness keeps its own scaffolding, tools, and context.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why explicit tools instead of prompt instructions?</h3>
            <p>Models are trained on tool calling. An agent given a <code>dispatch</code> tool reliably delegates; a prompt telling it to shell out to another CLI often gets ignored.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Is it production-ready?</h3>
            <p>The high-stakes paths are tested against real behavior rather than mocks: billing gating for every billing kind, workspace isolation actually dispatching away from your source tree, and subprocess timeouts and cleanup against real spawned processes. CI runs on Windows, macOS, and Linux.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="final">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Point your agent at every harness you own.</h2>
        <p>MIT licensed. Runs on your machine.</p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn primary" href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/harness-dispatch">npm install -g harness-dispatch</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span>harness-dispatch · MIT licensed</span>
      <span><a href="https://github.com/fstubner/harness-dispatch">GitHub</a></span>
    </div>
  </footer>
</Base>

<style>
  .transcript {
    margin-top: 40px;
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 28px 32px;
    max-width: 720px;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .t-user,
  .t-agent {
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 0 14px;
  }
  .t-user {
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .t-note {
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 10px 0;
  }
  .t-pending {
    color: var(--busy);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    margin: 8px 0 8px 14px;
  }
  .transcript > :last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .section-note {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .surface-title {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }
</style>
